#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "af255938-10ff-5169-9c5d-eac817354465" :question/math-academy-id "e-635" :question/problem "Find the integral expression corresponding to the area of the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sin(5\\theta)$, represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66062568cedc1b46822dd7e0e53779482f1923d621b47d29f38141ff770b4498.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = \\sin(5\\theta)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}(\\sin (5θ))^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sin^{2} (5θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.\n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or\n\n$$\n\\sin(5\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n5\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "c4c49c0d-865a-55d1-8194-35dda3907269" :question/math-academy-id "e-636" :question/problem "Find the interval for $\\theta$ corresponding to the first petal of the polar curve $r = 1+\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95138031d082cfc739c2c2569af9a9ea5c63d4b6990237b8267c4b2fe2481b09.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 1+\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}(1 + \\sqrt{2}\\cos (2θ + \\frac{π}{2}))^{2}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.\n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + \\sqrt{2}\\cos (2θ + \\frac{π}{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\cos (2θ + \\frac{π}{2}) &= -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for\n\n$$\n2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\in[-\\pi,\\pi)\n$$\n\n$$\n2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = -\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad 2\\theta = -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta = -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/id #uuid "3f355cb7-222f-5992-a212-f1b86ae37ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9541" :question/problem "Find the interval for $\\theta$ corresponding to the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta)$, represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e401186103f1cb3b060b99037bf5af07e873c191324759627275d77d235688c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}(\\sqrt{2}\\cos (6θ))^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\cos^{2} (6θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.\n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta\\in[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n6\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/12} \\cos^2(6\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90778680-dc00-5d45-9c7d-00a69ac3ac10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}\\cos (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102109} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f09940-0ee6-5e4f-81ea-72cb433df217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/6}^{π/2}\\cos^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102110} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dd63a4-a461-5b97-b8b8-c5fbcb516e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/6}\\cos^{2} (θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102111} {:answer/id #uuid "24142f49-20ed-5f35-8d27-f69bccac258e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/6}^{π}\\cos^{2} (3 + θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102112} {:answer/id #uuid "305176f4-e8d6-5b6f-901a-eb926c9b48b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}\\cos^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102113}] :answer-field/id #uuid "57276f9e-09b6-5a62-a9d8-da3b98983001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102108}] :question/id #uuid "a6c70642-adfc-517d-8652-a26cc7fbca82" :question/math-academy-id "q-124423" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c296052cfe60b35b326029afa1efb610ce32e4e2fb3ae0d63f24e437c3bbfb2.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sqrt{2}\\cos (3θ)$, represented by the shaded region shown above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a971f1b3-cc21-5e87-81f1-bccf4d00175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102765} {:answer/id #uuid "c89b8c5b-5b83-5528-90e2-d53f50978cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/3}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102766} {:answer/id #uuid "0edd856d-2986-5049-a4c5-0cd60d5b33a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/3}^{π/3}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102767} {:answer/id #uuid "38ceb0b5-0513-5180-9334-eb22d4bd2cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/3}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102768} {:answer/id #uuid "43897146-d18d-52ef-b148-5e9f9862d981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102769}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d21499a7-d8b9-5197-a6bf-e3d9bd7a4181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102764}] :question/id #uuid "db057c50-119e-5372-a829-3c2e39e59b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20905" :question/problem "![](images/01/01a371be4c5a555b0d3790431111468acfed1471d16c4c4c305bcfe311ce3576.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sin (3θ)$, represented by the shaded region shown above?"}]}
